# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-size/blob/main/src/libraries/actions/Liquidate.sol#L98
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-size/blob/main/src/libraries/actions/Liquidate.sol#L112


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The liquidator is benefitted at the expense of the Borrower and the Protocol because the `liquidatorReward` is roundedUp, and the `protocolProfitCollateralToken` is rounded down.

## Proof of Concept
When doing a profitable liquidation and calculating the liquidator and protocol rewards, the rounding of the computation benefits the liquidator at the expense of the borrower and the protocol.

[`liquidatorReward` is roundedUp](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-size/blob/main/src/libraries/actions/Liquidate.sol#L98), whilst [`protocolProfitCollateralToken` is rounded down](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-size/blob/main/src/libraries/actions/Liquidate.sol#L112). Which that means the liquidator gets its reward to be bigger than what it should, those extra rewards are taken from the leftover collateral, which it is distributed between the borrower and the protocol's profit.

```
function executeLiquidate(State storage state, LiquidateParams calldata params)
    ...
{
    ...
    // profitable liquidation
    if (assignedCollateral > debtInCollateralToken) {
        uint256 liquidatorReward = Math.min(
            assignedCollateral - debtInCollateralToken,
            //@audit-issue => Rounding up the liquidatorReward takes collateral from the borrower and the protocolProfit
            Math.mulDivUp(debtPosition.futureValue, state.feeConfig.liquidationRewardPercent, PERCENT)
        );
        liquidatorProfitCollateralToken = debtInCollateralToken + liquidatorReward;

        // split the remaining collateral between the protocol and the borrower, capped by the crLiquidation
        uint256 collateralRemainder = assignedCollateral - liquidatorProfitCollateralToken;

        ...

        collateralRemainder = Math.min(collateralRemainder, collateralRemainderCap);

        //@audit-issue => Rounding down the protocolProfit causes the protocol to take less comission than what it could
        protocolProfitCollateralToken = Math.mulDivDown(collateralRemainder, collateralProtocolPercent, PERCENT);
    } else {
        ...
    }

    ...
}

```

## Tools Used
Manual Audit

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Invert the rounding when computing `liquidatorReward` and `protocolProfitCollateralToken`. Opt to benefit the Borrower and the Protocol, instead of benefiting the Liquidator.

```
function executeLiquidate(State storage state, LiquidateParams calldata params)
    ...
{
    ...
    // profitable liquidation
    if (assignedCollateral > debtInCollateralToken) {
        uint256 liquidatorReward = Math.min(
            assignedCollateral - debtInCollateralToken,
-           Math.mulDivUp(debtPosition.futureValue, state.feeConfig.liquidationRewardPercent, PERCENT)
+           Math.mulDivDown(debtPosition.futureValue, state.feeConfig.liquidationRewardPercent, PERCENT)
        );
        liquidatorProfitCollateralToken = debtInCollateralToken + liquidatorReward;

        // split the remaining collateral between the protocol and the borrower, capped by the crLiquidation
        uint256 collateralRemainder = assignedCollateral - liquidatorProfitCollateralToken;

        ...

        collateralRemainder = Math.min(collateralRemainder, collateralRemainderCap);

-       protocolProfitCollateralToken = Math.mulDivDown(collateralRemainder, collateralProtocolPercent, PERCENT);
+       protocolProfitCollateralToken = Math.mulDivUp(collateralRemainder, collateralProtocolPercent, PERCENT);
    } else {
        ...
    }

    ...
}

```



