# Vulnerability details

## Impact
First and foremost, the `YieldCurve` accepting `int256[]` for `aprs` is directly contradicting the code in the interface of `ISize` like [here](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-size/blob/8850e25fb088898e9cf86f9be1c401ad155bea86/src/interfaces/ISize.sol#L66) and [here](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-size/blob/8850e25fb088898e9cf86f9be1c401ad155bea86/src/interfaces/ISize.sol#L74). If accepting `int256[]`, that means some part of the yield curve can have negative values, which does not make sense in the lending business at all. Not any part of the `YieldCurve` should be negative no matter what the associated `tenor` is. That is counter-intuitive.

Moreover, if a user used some negative `aprs` to set the `YieldCurve` when he calls `buyCreditLimit` or `sellCreditLimit`, his limit order actually can pass the `validate` step and the `execute` step, so his loan/borrow offer will be listed just fine. However, there a chance his offer cannot be fulfilled, because when there is a match market order, eventually the code will call the `getAPRByTenor`. If the returned value happens to be in the negative territory, the call will just revert because `apr` in the Market order is defined as `uint256`. Note that such kind of **revert due to arithmetic underflow is not something intended to happen** at this step (it's not like a failed validation revert), it should be caught and reverted in the earlier `validate` step when the user tries to list his offer, and do not allow such problematic offers to be listed.

